=== task: code medium
title: Insert into a static array
prompt: Write `insert_static(slots, count, i, x)`: if the array is full, change nothing and return `count`. Otherwise move the used slots from index `count - 1` back to `i` one place right (start at the end), put `x` in slot `i`, and return the new count. The tests call your function, then show its answer and the slots.
starter:
```python
def insert_static(slots, count, i, x):
    if count == len(slots):
        return count
    # your code here
    return count + 1
```
solution:
```python
def insert_static(slots, count, i, x):
    if count == len(slots):
        return count
    j = count
    while j > i:
        slots[j] = slots[j - 1]
        j -= 1
    slots[i] = x
    return count + 1
```
tests:
- (lambda s: [insert_static(s, 2, 1, 20), s])([10, 30, None])
- (lambda s: [insert_static(s, 2, 0, 9), s])([1, 2])
- (lambda s: [insert_static(s, 0, 0, "a"), s])([None, None])
- (lambda s: [insert_static(s, 1, 1, 6), s])([5, None, None])
- (lambda s: [insert_static(s, 2, 0, "a"), s])(["b", "c", None])
fail[1]: 30 moves right to slot 2, then 20 goes into slot 1: `[10, 20, 30]`.
fail[2]: The array is full: nothing changes.
fail[4]: Inserting at index `count` is like appending: nothing moves.
fail[5]: At the front, both items move right: `["a", "b", "c"]`.
forbid[insert(]: Move the slots yourself instead of using `insert`.
hint: Start at the first free slot: `j = count`.
hint: While `j > i`: `slots[j] = slots[j - 1]` and `j -= 1`.
hint: Then `slots[i] = x`.
right: count − i moves and one write: O(n) at the front, O(1) at the end. Starting from the end means no item is overwritten before it has moved.
wrong: From `j = count` down to `i + 1`, copy each slot from its left neighbour; then write `x` into slot `i`.

=== task: code medium
title: Delete from a static array
prompt: Write `delete_static(slots, count, i)`: move the used slots after index `i` one place left, set the last used slot (index `count - 1`) back to `None`, and return the new count. The tests call your function, then show its answer and the slots.
starter:
```python
def delete_static(slots, count, i):
    # your code here
    return count - 1
```
solution:
```python
def delete_static(slots, count, i):
    j = i
    while j < count - 1:
        slots[j] = slots[j + 1]
        j += 1
    slots[count - 1] = None
    return count - 1
```
tests:
- (lambda s: [delete_static(s, 3, 1), s])([10, 20, 30, None])
- (lambda s: [delete_static(s, 1, 0), s])([5, None])
- (lambda s: [delete_static(s, 3, 2), s])([1, 2, 3])
- (lambda s: [delete_static(s, 3, 0), s])([1, 2, 3])
fail[1]: 30 moves into slot 1, and slot 2 is freed: `[10, 30, None, None]`.
fail[2]: The only item is deleted: its slot becomes None, and the count becomes 0.
fail[4]: 2 and 3 move left, and the last used slot is freed: `[2, 3, None]`.
forbid[pop(]: A static array keeps all its slots: free the last one with `None` instead of removing it.
forbid[del slots[]: A static array keeps all its slots: free the last one with `None` instead of deleting it.
hint: Start at the gap: `j = i`. While `j < count - 1`: `slots[j] = slots[j + 1]`.
hint: The last used slot now holds a leftover copy.
hint: `slots[count - 1] = None`, then return `count - 1`.
right: count − i − 1 moves and one clear: O(n) at the front, O(1) at the end. The array keeps all its slots; only the count goes down.
wrong: Shift the slots after i one place left, set slot `count - 1` to None, and return `count - 1`.

=== task: predict medium
