=== task: mcq hard
title: An insert anywhere
prompt: Items are inserted into a static array holding n items, at an index chosen at random from 0 to n. About how many items move on average, and what's the Big O?
- About 1 item, so O(1) :: Only an insert at the very end moves nothing. Most inserts move many items.
* About n / 2 items, so O(n) :: Right. Index i moves n − i items, and on average that's about half the array.
- About log n items, so O(log n) :: Nothing is halved: the moves are spread evenly from 0 to n.
- About n² items, so O(n²) :: One insert moves at most n items.
hint: Insert at index i moves n − i items, for i from 0 to n.
hint: The average of 0, 1, ..., n is n / 2.
right: n / 2 on average, which is still O(n): inserting into the middle of an array is a linear-time job.
wrong: The moves range from n (at the front) down to 0 (at the end), averaging about n / 2: O(n).

=== task: multi hard
title: True about static arrays
prompt: Choose **every** true sentence.
* The next free slot is always at index count :: Right. The used slots are 0 to count − 1.
* A static array's capacity never changes :: Right. That's what makes it static.
* Deleting sets the last used slot back to None, but the array keeps all its slots :: Right. Only the count goes down.
- Appending to a full static array makes it bigger :: It can't grow: the append has to be refused (or the array copied into a bigger one).
- Reading slot i costs more when i is large :: Every slot is found with the same address arithmetic: O(1).
- Inserting at index count moves every item :: Index count is the first free slot: nothing moves.
hint: Think about capacity, count, and where the free slots are.
hint: Three of them are true.
right: The next slot is at count, the capacity is fixed, and deleting only frees a slot. A full array can't grow, reads are O(1) everywhere, and inserting at count is an append.
wrong: True: next slot at count, fixed capacity, deletes free a slot. False: full arrays don't grow, large indexes aren't slower, inserting at count moves nothing.

